findings.body:
Switching from `execFile` (argv array, no shell) to `exec` with a template string lets attacker-controlled `src` / `filter` run arbitrary shell commands. `filter` is unquoted; `src` is only double-quoted, so `\"`, `$()`, and backticks still break out.

ImageMagick `-filter` is a normal CLI argument — no shell is required. Restore `execFile` and pass the filter as its own argv element:

```suggestion
import { execFile } from "child_process";

export function resize(src: string, filter: string, w: number): Promise<string> {
  return new Promise((res, rej) => {
    execFile("convert", [src, "-filter", filter, "-resize", `${w}x`, "/tmp/out.png"], (e) => e ? rej(e) : res("/tmp/out.png"));
  });
}
```

Also validate `filter` against an allowlist of known ImageMagick filter names if the input is user-facing.
